WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.642
Lesson 1 becomes much clearer when magnetic flux is treated as a measurable field-through-area story and induced emf is treated as a rate-of-change story.

2
00:00:05.642 --> 00:00:11.284
Flux means field through an area.

3
00:00:11.284 --> 00:00:16.925
The loop intercepts magnetic field through its surface.

4
00:00:16.925 --> 00:00:22.567
Flux linkage means turns matter too.

5
00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:28.209
More turns mean the same flux change can produce a bigger linked effect.

6
00:00:28.209 --> 00:00:33.851
Faraday lens means change per time.

7
00:00:33.851 --> 00:00:39.493
Induced emf depends on how quickly the linked flux changes.

8
00:00:39.493 --> 00:00:45.135
The weak move is to say induction happens because a magnet is present, even when the flux through the loop is staying constant.

9
00:00:45.135 --> 00:00:50.776
Once the changing quantity is named properly, the worked example becomes a bookkeeping exercise instead of vague magnet language.

10
00:00:50.776 --> 00:00:56.418
No changing flux linkage means no induced emf.

11
00:00:56.418 --> 00:01:02.060
Keep this takeaway in view: No changing flux linkage means no induced emf.

12
00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:06.321
This diagram matters because it makes the sources of flux change visible.

13
00:01:06.321 --> 00:01:10.582
Field strength can change, loop area can change, and orientation can change.

14
00:01:10.582 --> 00:01:14.843
Field strength means more or less B.

15
00:01:14.843 --> 00:01:19.104
A stronger field sends more magnetic thread through the loop.

16
00:01:19.104 --> 00:01:23.365
Loop area means bigger or smaller window.

17
00:01:23.365 --> 00:01:27.626
Changing the area changes how much field is captured.

18
00:01:27.626 --> 00:01:31.887
Tilt means more face-on or edge-on.

19
00:01:31.887 --> 00:01:36.148
Changing orientation changes the effective field through the loop.

20
00:01:36.148 --> 00:01:40.409
Flux is geometric and physical, not just symbolic.

21
00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:44.670
Keep this takeaway in view: Flux is geometric and physical, not just symbolic.

22
00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.809
A window-in-the-rain analogy works because the amount passing through depends on the opening, the direction, and how those features change over time.

23
00:01:48.809 --> 00:01:52.948
More rain density means stronger field.

24
00:01:52.948 --> 00:01:57.087
A heavier rain corresponds to a stronger field.

25
00:01:57.087 --> 00:02:01.226
Bigger window means larger area.

26
00:02:01.226 --> 00:02:05.364
A larger opening lets more through.

27
00:02:05.364 --> 00:02:09.503
Tilted window means orientation effect.

28
00:02:09.503 --> 00:02:13.642
A tilted opening catches less straight-through flow.

29
00:02:13.642 --> 00:02:17.781
Flux is about what threads through the loop area.

30
00:02:17.781 --> 00:02:21.920
Keep this takeaway in view: Flux is about what threads through the loop area.

31
00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.653
The formula board is strongest when the learner reads flux linkage change first and induced emf second.

32
00:02:26.653 --> 00:02:31.385
First move means identify the flux change.

33
00:02:31.385 --> 00:02:36.118
Ask what changed: B, A, or orientation.

34
00:02:36.118 --> 00:02:40.851
Second move means scale by turns.

35
00:02:40.851 --> 00:02:45.584
Multiple turns multiply the linked change.

36
00:02:45.584 --> 00:02:50.316
Third move means divide by time.

37
00:02:50.316 --> 00:02:55.049
Faster change gives larger induced emf.

38
00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:59.782
Keep these relations visible: induced emf = N delta(Phi) / delta(t); Phi = B A cos(theta); and no flux change means no induced emf.

39
00:02:59.782 --> 00:03:04.515
Use the geometry to identify the flux change, then use turns and time to scale the induced emf.

40
00:03:04.515 --> 00:03:09.247
Induced emf is a rate-of-change quantity.

41
00:03:09.247 --> 00:03:13.980
Keep this takeaway in view: Induced emf is a rate-of-change quantity.

42
00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.647
A dependable Lesson 1 routine starts by naming the changing physical cause, then converts that cause into a flux change before using the induction formula.

43
00:03:18.647 --> 00:03:23.313
Step 1 means name the change.

44
00:03:23.313 --> 00:03:27.980
Field, area, orientation, or turns?

45
00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:32.647
Step 2 means find delta Phi.

46
00:03:32.647 --> 00:03:37.313
Work out the change in flux per turn.

47
00:03:37.313 --> 00:03:41.980
Step 3 means apply N and delta t.

48
00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:46.647
Turn flux change into induced emf.

49
00:03:46.647 --> 00:03:51.313
Name the change before you touch the formula.

50
00:03:51.313 --> 00:03:55.980
Keep this takeaway in view: Name the change before you touch the formula.

51
00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:59.320
If the flux through the loop stays constant, there is nothing for the induced emf to react to.

52
00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.660
Flux present means can still be steady.

53
00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:06.000
A loop can sit in a field without producing induction.

54
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.340
No change means no induced emf.

55
00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:12.680
Faraday induction reacts to change, not to mere presence.

56
00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:16.020
Lesson gain means zero case.

57
00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.360
This is the easiest reasonableness check in the topic.

58
00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.700
Steady flux gives zero induced emf.

59
00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:26.040
Keep this takeaway in view: Steady flux gives zero induced emf.

60
00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:30.063
A coil with more turns does not need a bigger flux change to get a bigger induced emf.

61
00:04:30.063 --> 00:04:34.086
It needs the same change linked more times.

62
00:04:34.086 --> 00:04:38.109
One loop means single flux change.

63
00:04:38.109 --> 00:04:42.132
This is the per-turn picture.

64
00:04:42.132 --> 00:04:46.155
Many turns means repeated linkage.

65
00:04:46.155 --> 00:04:50.178
The same change counts through each turn.

66
00:04:50.178 --> 00:04:54.201
Lesson gain means N scales emf.

67
00:04:54.201 --> 00:04:58.224
Turns multiply the linked change before time is applied.

68
00:04:58.224 --> 00:05:02.247
Flux linkage is the right bridge between loop geometry and coil emf.

69
00:05:02.247 --> 00:05:06.270
Keep this takeaway in view: Flux linkage is the right bridge between loop geometry and coil emf.

70
00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:10.537
Orientation matters because a loop facing the field fully is not intercepting the same field-thread as a loop turned edge-on.

71
00:05:10.537 --> 00:05:14.803
Face-on means larger through-flow.

72
00:05:14.803 --> 00:05:19.070
More field threads the loop surface.

73
00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:23.337
Edge-on means smaller through-flow.

74
00:05:23.337 --> 00:05:27.603
Flux can shrink all the way to zero.

75
00:05:27.603 --> 00:05:31.870
Lesson gain means rotation creates induction.

76
00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:36.137
Changing angle is enough to create flux change.

77
00:05:36.137 --> 00:05:40.403
Tilt changes flux and can therefore create induction.

78
00:05:40.403 --> 00:05:44.670
Keep this takeaway in view: Tilt changes flux and can therefore create induction.

79
00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:47.820
Use this checkpoint to test whether change-versus-presence is now clear.

80
00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.970
Question means steady flux.

81
00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:54.120
What happens to the induced emf?

82
00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.270
Route means Faraday responds to change.

83
00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:00.420
No change means the rate term is zero.

84
00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:03.570
Answer check means zero induced emf.

85
00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.720
A steady flux does not induce emf.

86
00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.870
Induction begins with change.

87
00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:13.020
Keep this takeaway in view: Induction begins with change.

88
00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:17.775
Carry one route into the worked example.

89
00:06:17.775 --> 00:06:22.530
Name the changing cause, calculate the flux change per turn, multiply by turns, and divide by the time interval.

90
00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:27.285
Step 1 means find delta Phi.

91
00:06:27.285 --> 00:06:32.040
Start with the geometry or field change.

92
00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:36.795
Step 2 means multiply by N.

93
00:06:36.795 --> 00:06:41.550
Count how many turns link the change.

94
00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:46.305
Step 3 means divide by delta t.

95
00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:51.060
Faster change means bigger induced emf.

96
00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:55.815
Keep these relations visible: find delta Phi from the physical change; multiply by N for flux linkage change; and divide by delta t for induced emf.

97
00:06:55.815 --> 00:07:00.570
Keep the no-change case in mind as a quick check before calculation.

98
00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:05.325
Flux change first, emf second.

99
00:07:05.325 --> 00:07:10.080
Keep this takeaway in view: Flux change first, emf second.
